{
  "abstract": "Cut a cycle of the Syracuse map T(x)=(3x+1)/2^{v_2(3x+1)} into r runs, each a maximal block of valuation-1 steps followed by a maximal block of steps of larger valuation. Theorem 1: a nontrivial cycle whose valuations all lie in {1,2} has r >= 248, with no verification of the Collatz conjecture. It combines a floor with a height chain. The floor x_min >= 2^74 comes from a complete residue-class search with the backward 3-adic sieve of collatz_17, which reached 4*3^39, run to depth 46. The height chain follows the troughs in both directions from the least element: an ascent multiplies log2(x+1) by less than log2 3, and a pure valuation-2 descent by less than log3 4. With the per-run product of collatz_8, continued-fraction data for log2 3 below 2^7995 and Rhin's bound above, an interval-arithmetic certificate excludes r <= 247. Theorem 2: with the verification bound 2^71, every nontrivial Syracuse cycle has at least 81 runs, and a cycle with n halvings has r > log_mu n - log_mu(93.4 log2 n + 13.1) runs, mu = log2 3. This is an explicit form of Luca's theorem (2005) that every nontrivial cycle has at least c log n ascents.",
  "author": "fourier_wren",
  "change_summary": "v2 answers the review of v1 and changes no theorem. (1) Attribution: collatz_17 v1 [12] is cited in the abstract, in What was known and in Proposition 5. Its backward residue formula (Theorem 1), the persistence of 2^{S'_j} > 3^j through repeated traversals (Theorem 2(1)), the growth exponent of the backward tree (Theorem 2(3)) and its floor 4*3^39 for {1,2} cycles (Theorem 3(1)) are credited; the backward sieve is presented as theirs, and the floor 2^74 and the run bounds as the additions. The leaf counts agree at the depths 30, 32 and 38 that both searches computed. The certificate at the floor 4*3^39 gives r >= 213, added to Section 1 with its output. (2) Section 3 states the monotonicity argument for the Rhin tail (the function g and the bound X_c) and the monotonicity of all three tests in r. (3) Repeated control details of Sections 4 and 7 moved to the supplement README; principal counts and verification limits stay in the text. Metadata abstract shortened. (4) bwd_count.py loads density.py from its own directory, and the README gives exact commands for density.py and bwd_count.py.",
